{
  "esm": "1.2.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "expand-sourcetype-runspec",
    "description": "PLAN.md §7.5 step 7d, the SOURCE TYPE dimension at width five: the `expand-sourcetype` run computed from the DEFAULT DATABASE through `moves-onroad.esm`. Source types 11 (motorcycle), 21 (passenger car), 31 (passenger truck), 51 (refuse truck) and 62 (combination long-haul truck) -- two light-duty, one heavy-duty vocational, one long-haul and a motorcycle, which is as far apart as this database's physics gets.\n\nWHAT IT DISCRIMINATES over `expand-fueltype-diesel`, which is the same axis at width two.\n\n* FIVE DRIVE CYCLES RATHER THAN TWO, and five that disagree. `sourceusetypephysics` gives each source type its own rolling, rotating and drag terms and its own fixed mass factor, so the vehicle-specific power of a drive-schedule second, the operating mode it bins into and the speed-bin brackets differ across all five. A motorcycle and a combination long-haul truck do not share an operating-mode distribution.\n\n* A COHORT COUNT THAT IS A SUM OVER FIVE UNEQUAL TERMS. 430 rows: 41 + 125 + 125 + 89 + 50. Source type 11 carries one fuel and 41 model years; 21 and 31 carry four fuels each and reach the full 125; 51 and 62 carry fuels the database has fewer sample vehicles for. 656 candidates in the grid, 430 survivors, and no declaration anywhere says 430 -- `cohortSurvivorCount` recomputes it and `tools/check-runspec-shape.py` holds the emitted relation to it.\n\nTHE TEN SELECTIONS, ONE BINDING AND THE ORACLE are everything this document carries, and it differs from `runspecs/expand-fueltype-diesel.esm` in eleven `url_template`s alone.\n\nWHY THIS DOCUMENT IS IN `runspecs/pending/` AND NOT `runspecs/`, as of 2026-09-18, and for a DIFFERENT reason since the source bin landed. It is complete and it validates; the model does not yet reproduce it, for one reason that is neither the source-type axis nor the source bin. The vehicle physics `sourceusetypephysics` holds is keyed by (sourceTypeID, regClassID, model-year range), and this document's drive cycle is per SOURCE TYPE: `phys_rollingTermA` and the rest are a `sum_product` over the rows a source type selects. Source type 21 selects ONE, so the distinction was invisible for every run before this one. This run selects the two source types that have one row apiece (11 and 21) and three that do not: 31 selects three, 51 fifteen and 62 twenty. The model's own guard says so -- `run_physicsRowsSelected` is per source type and its description says the day it is not 1 `dss_vsp` is the sum of several vehicles' physics rather than one's.\n\nWHAT IS ALREADY PROVEN BY RUNNING IT, which is why it is checked in rather than deleted. It is the width-five companion to `expand-fueltype-diesel`, which is the same claim at width two and where the measurements are: there the activity and the source-bin distribution are both exact for both source types and only the operating-mode distribution is wrong. This document is written and validating so that the step which fixes that has a run to widen to, rather than a run to write afterwards.\n\nWHAT IT WAITS ON: the drive cycle becoming per PHYSICS ROW rather than per source type. `sourceusetypephysics` has 178 rows over 13 source types, 1 for a motorcycle or a passenger car and 15 to 21 for a heavy truck; this run selects 4 of them and `expand-sourcetype` selects 40. Everything from `phys_isSelected` down to `pp_W` carries a `runspecsourcetype_rows` axis today and would carry that one instead, which makes it the same promotion 7d did for the source type, one key finer and over the model's largest relational step. PLAN.md §7.3.6 has the measurement.",
    "authors": [
      "EarthSciML Authors and Contributors"
    ],
    "tags": [
      "runspec",
      "onroad",
      "default-database",
      "expand-day"
    ]
  },
